decision B8.10).\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2168:decision B8.11).\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2180:|                     | until shutdown and its destructors  | (B8.12)          |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2258:| (8.1)               | `$GLOBALS = ...` a compile error.   |                  |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2464:standing rule of the ledger's PHP section (owner decision B8.13).\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2470:1. (B8.1) Reference runtime PHP 8.3 at a pinned patch release (recommended)\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2495:10. (B8.10) Optional absent includes stay refused at publication until the\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2497:11. (B8.11) Adopt the support-profile statement and the corner table of\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2499:12. (B8.12) C1 cycle collection scheduled after the reclamation work rather\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2501:13. (B8.13) The blocking rule of section 17.3 binding for `yang.php` landings\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:305:so no published image is stranded. `yin.vm.linker.md` section 8.1 makes\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:63:| jolt Python parser has no deterministic work budget: time inside the input and nesting axes is unbounded (a `1+` chain is quadratic in the pinned runtime; an SLL lookahead over a `1*` chain grows faster; a few-kilobyte source holds the parse thread for minutes), and no refusal is keyed on elapsed time (a refusal is a function of the source) | spec §7.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c implemented (2026-11-01): one `work` counter charged at three units — every rule entry, every `getEpsilonTarget` closure step and every consumed token (the unit that sees the `1+` quadratic: prediction's DFA walk does not accept until its statement ends, so every loop decision re-consumes the rest of the statement — 64,024,002 consumptions for a 16,002-token 8,000-operator chain; a counter of closure steps and rule entries alone sees the chain's work linear, measured) — refused as status -7 `work <measured> <bound>` (the same status as the nesting guards, not -8: the message names the axis), `{:diagnostic :parser-budget :axis :work}`; no unit throws inside a full-context prediction (its consume would unwind past the reach computation the leak fix protects); checked again at every prediction's entry and after recognition; a work refusal resets the worker's parser DFA so a refused source refuses on retry; `kMaxWork` 600,000,000, calibrated (`bb calibrate:jolt-j5 --work`, build/j5b/work.edn) from the measured maximum over the whole parsing corpus — the gated 20,000-operator `1+` chain at 400,129,567 units is the floor — with the adversarial curves above it (`1*`x4,000 about 8.1e8, refuses mid-walk; `1+` past 24,491 operators refuses); the corpus, the at-bound nesting shapes, the 4 MiB flat inputs and the gated flat sizes parse unchanged, zero parity pins. Divergences from the row's plan recorded: the 10 s refusal walls were infeasible with the floor the existing gates set (refusing at 600,000,000 units costs the time those units cost — about 20 s for the `1+` family, minutes-becoming-tens-of-seconds for the `1*` walk), and the bound is not per-byte (per-byte cannot separate a 24 KB `1*` chain from a 24 KB legitimate source). The lexer's per-NEWLINE constant (two `std::regex` constructions per newline in the generated lexer base) is linear in work but unbounded in wall: disclosed in §7.6, not bounded. Linux evidence pending (the orchestrator closes this row); precondition for parsing remotely sourced Python (J6) | P2 |\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:350:| yang.clojure: the slice-9 kernel differential gate is honestly blocked at **107/165 credited (39 direct + 68 named integrations), 58 unexercised admitted arities**, 100 agreements, 2 recorded deviations (`elab-after-qed`, `check-after-qed`), 2 blocked fixtures, 2 declared upstream failures; the earlier 145/165-and-20 accounting predated the common-call reconciliation and under-counted (38 named-integration trace paths no longer exist) | hardening-3 H3-0 (the architect's finding: the deviation classifier required a bare closure where the reconciled compiler publishes the admitted §8.10 descriptor) | yang.clojure | H3-0 repaired the bookkeeping (versioned slice9_declarations.edn drives a representation-aware classifier with declared-call, message and script revalidation; the writer carries declared dispositions; a writer round-trip test replays the checked-in evidence) — repair, not closure; H3-1..H3-5 close the 58 per the approved hardening-3 plan | P2 |\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:397:## 2026-10-29 19:5x +0700 — the session-id collision lesson logged; the cross-language demo and the 8.10 spec distinction recorded\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:400:Done: The owner asked two questions that produced recorded findings: (1) why no terminals are visible (the delegates run as background processes writing to collab/ logs, not interactive terminals; the shiva lanes run in tmux on the remote server), and (2) whether the section 8.10 cross-language calling convention spec is unnecessary given that cross-language calls already work. The answer to (2): no — the demo proved the mechanism (the substrate routes calls correctly), but the spec defines the semantics (null sentinels, number kinds, opaque handles, references, exceptions, cleanup, ownership). The calls work at the engine level; the contract for what values mean at the boundary didn't exist until the spec defined it. The distinction: \"the pipe works\" vs \"what flows through the pipe and what happens when it leaks.\"\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:402:Verification: the cross-language demo (test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj, landed 2e1d09e6) proves the mechanism; the section 8.10 spec defines the semantics.\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:646:Decisions: the handoff artifact is this log entry — the yang.call API surface (entry, eligible?, invocation, invocation-with, valid-invocation?, condition, thrown, transfer, invoke, invoke-with), the dependency addresses (yang.core.call, yang.core.uast, yang.core.prelude, yin.vm, yin.vm.module), and the convention spec (yang.antlr.md section 8.10, committed eb8030e8, kimi-approved).\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:685:Done: (1) The peer python-orchestrator note (collab/1793610000000-...) claimed the section 8.10.11 gap for TS/PHP; codex verified it READ-ONLY (collab/orch-lane/1793630000000-engineer-crosslang-gap-verify.gpt-6-luna.stdout.log): PARTIAL — the rewrites (cross_lang_test.clj:110-137 postwalk) and synthetic cells (call_test.cljc:202-260 inline fns) CONFIRMED with line evidence; the \"bypass\" framing too broad (the calls already route through invoke-with/:yang.call/entry); no qualifying real-source x convention test for TS/PHP anywhere. (2) The TS S11 round landed (cccebf3e on yang-ts-t3, PUSHED and shiva-verified: JVM subsystem 18 ns / 103 tests / 0 failures in the fresh ts3b clone): direct TS->PHP source calls pass on JVM and Node; the higher-order callback leg was blocked because PHP's resolver rejected TS cells. (3) The PHP conformance round landed (d145085b: unchanged PHP source calling a named TS export + the stored-called-returned closure leg; PHP closures publish a yang.call entry on the closure value) and the foreign-admission round landed (edc50134: PHP admits conforming foreign callable cells through the shared resolver; non-conforming cells keep the undefined-function diagnostic; the PHP-side callback identity test passes). Both PUSHED. (4) The foreach_016 defect fixed (3321ecee: by-ref foreach cursor preserved across array_unshift reindexing; the pin matched on all four VMs at 481 ms). (5) The e2e red triaged by reproduction at the tip: the 115/271 PHPT selection mismatches are the LONG-STANDING S10 backlog (foreach by-ref, gc, unset CV, named-params families); the refusals-are-named FOO diagnostic regression is REAL (echo FOO produces nil instead of :yang.php/unsupported/:constant — likely the P6 constants work); plus one error. The triage round (manifest bookkeeping for the selection, the FOO fix, the error, the gc_013 disposition) dispatched to luna. (6) gc_013 oracle cross-check run by the seat on shiva (docker php:8.3-cli, the pinned source): real PHP outputs bool(true)/ok — the PIN IS CORRECT; the mismatch is the engine's cycle-collection semantics -> a named known-gap row. (7) The lane lessons: the codex resume writer-lock recursed (the r2 nuance dispatch failed with thread-store conflict; sol's r1 had already converged correctly, so no re-dispatch needed); scratch-clone focused runs need clojure -M:test; the -e :slow flag EXCLUDES slow tests (lane7 forgot it and ran the 259 s gc_013 stress case); an orphaned 100%-CPU test-runner survived a tmux kill-session and burned 2.5 h before the seat killed it.\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:729:Next: the P7-a execution-budget round (kimi's ruled contract, now unblocked by the matrix); the P4 S9-S12 and P6 S6+ plan steps; the route-status of remaining §8.10.11 evidence (the higher-order legs for ts<->php exist inside the matrix now).\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2956:Done: Chartered and drove the code-as-tuples design unit per the owner's seven rulings. Author claude-fable-5.1 (fresh session 8677b374, then resumed for revision), reviewer gpt-6-astra (codex thread 01a09fcc), independence preserved (Claude↔GPT), orchestrator verified all rounds' surprising claims against source (100% held). Round 1 draft: docs/design/yin.vm.tuples.md 780 lines — three-layer stance (objects/refs/projection), full AST grammar table, boundary calls (:vm/store-update excluded; :stream/close kept with walker gaining the arm), whole-tree addressing, querying, VM load paths, ledger-as-refs, honest migration + blockers. Round 1 review: REJECT, 12 P1s, convergent (central decision, grammar inventory, boundary calls, addressing, query mechanics, migration counts all verified sound; defects all integration-protocol: keyword store keys vs sym kind, ledger rows illegal under local-datom?, runtime bookkeeping written into the AST node under :frame, dependency queries missing parked ids, occurrence identity, macro admission, lowering revision, encoder blocker scope). Round 2 revision: all 12 addressed, none overruled, 780→1132 lines — frame schema table (§2.6), ledger records as content-addressed objects entering as local event entities (§8.1-8.2, legal under local-datom?/pad-datom, no reserved-id allocation), occurrence keys [root-address path], macro declaration carriage (§8.5), stamped derivation records, shared validator + UCF §7.3.4 projection-primary supersession. Round 2 re-review (after a codex usage-limit abort at ~21:45, auto-retried 00:12 via one-shot cron after reset): REJECT again — 5/12 resolved (frames, shared validator, ledger legality, reserved ops, as-of — as-of executed and passed), 1 regressed (encoder gate: the revision's \"reject lists\" restricted domain is itself unsound — (seq [1 2]) and metadata-carrying vectors still collide), 6 narrowed-not-resolved (store-key domain must be the full admitted data domain; plain-data result condition still admits effect descriptors since effect? = any map with :effect; dependency completion still uses any-environment satisfaction; occurrence identity lacks source/batch and expansion-event qualification; macro carriage lacks whole-batch admission/disconnected definitions/stray-mark rejection; derivation stamp must pin the lowering algorithm, not reuse UCF's execution-contract stamp; segment effect query returns mnemonics vs the :stream/put vocabulary). Central representation decisions again endorsed: \"sound\", \"closer to implementable\".\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:8483:medium), F2 (yang.antlr.md §8.1 contradiction), F3 (completion/encoder must refuse :cell-ref in slice 1), F4-F7 low/doc.\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:8485:D