r rules of §3.4 (both load paths), **and the §8.1 analysis\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files collab | rg '1794600000000|attribution|addendum'; cat docs/agents/routing-status.md; cat docs/agents/roles/architect.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files collab | rg '1794600000000|attribution|addendum'; cat docs/agents/routing-status.md; cat docs/agents/roles/architect.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-DRAFT.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\ncollab/orch-lane/1794600000000-engineer-roundA-p1-fixes.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-python/1793410000000-orchestrator-yang-python-attribution-note.md\\ncollab/dao-jing/1790012238375-architect-jing-cbor-eve-addendum.prompt.md\\ncollab/yin-vm/1790016536012-architect-debruijn-vm-revision-r4-addendum.prompt.md\\ncollab/yin-vm/1791990000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5c-r2.mutant-addendum.md\\n# Delegate routing status\\n\\nDurable record of owner-relayed delegate CLI/model availability and budget\\nstatus. No tool exposes this automatically (weekly quotas, provider outages,\\nspending caps) — the owner relays it in conversation, and it otherwise dies\\nwith that session. This file is what lets a different orchestrator seat, in a\\nfresh session with no shared history, know the current routing constraints\\nwithout re-asking or discovering them the hard way.\\n\\nAppend-only in spirit, like `docs/orchestrator-log.md`: when a status goes\\nstale or is superseded, add a new entry rather than editing the old one, so\\nthe history of what was true when stays visible. Unlike the work log, this\\nfile may be edited to trim entries that are long superseded and no longer\\nuseful context, since this is a status board, not a provenance record.\\n\\nEvery entry states: the destination, what the owner said, when, and how to\\napply it. Treat every entry here as a claim to re-verify (a quota resets, an\\noutage resolves), not as permanent authority — the same posture as the work\\nlog.\\n\\n## How to apply\\n\\nBefore routing a new dispatch to any metered/quota-based destination, check\\nthis file's most recent entry for that destination. If none exists or the\\nstated reset time has passed, the status is unknown — proceed normally, but\\nconsider a cheap sanity check (a trivial prompt) before committing a\\nreal task to a destination that failed recently. When the owner relays a new\\nstatus in conversation, add an entry here before continuing, so it survives\\npast the current session.\\n\\n## Current status\\n\\n2026-11-04 measured — qwen-code LIVE: Alibaba's `qwen` CLI configured on the Token Plan and verified\\nDistinct from the withdrawn claude-wrapper of this morning: `qwen` now means `@qwen-code/qwen-code@0.17.0` (nvm node v26.1.0 npm-global; a second mise install was found and removed today at the owner's request). The owner's key `sk-sp-H.LXYLM.f0Vo.…` is registered in ~/.qwen/settings.json env; the gateway is https://token-plan.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1 (region-bound: the key 403s at cn-beijing). Measured usable catalog: qwen3.8-max / qwen3.8-flash / qwen3.7-max / qwen3.6-flash / deepseek-v4-pro — everything else the pre-existing auto-written config listed is \\\"Access to model denied\\\" or \\\"Model not exist\\\". Headless probe `qwen -p \\\"...\\\" < /dev/null` returned OK rc=0. Session-ID mechanics not yet measured; do not invent them. Consequence: qwen is an available implementation destination NOW; parallel capacity qwen | kimi | glm | muse | codex. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (qwen3.8-flash).\\n\\n2026-11-04 measured then withdrawn — the `qwen` wrapper lane is REMOVED (name collision with Alibaba's `qwen-code` agent)\\nThe Model Studio Token Plan measurement stands (registered via `bl auth login --config token-plan`; Token Plan Anthropic endpoint https://token-plan.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com/apps/anthropic works end-to-end through a claude-family wrapper; measured catalog qwen3.8-max + qwen3.8-flash only; cn-beijing gateway 403s this key; `bl config agent --agent claude-code` is a global takeover — CAUTION stands). But the `~/.local/bin/qwen` wrapper was deleted the same day: \\\"qwen\\\" collides with Alibaba's separate `qwen-code` coding agent, making the name ambiguous as a delegate. NO qwen dispatch lane is installed; re-create one under a non-colliding name before routing anything here. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-04 owner request — measured: muse-spark-1.3 WORKS on the API-key lane (contributor mode on)\\nProbe on the laptop: `muse exec --trust-workspace --approval-mode never --model muse-spark-1.3` returned OK rc=0 in a fresh trusted dir. `muse usage` reports \\\"No subscription allowance (api_key)\\\" — the run is billed through the persisted API key (~/.config/muse/api-key, keychain auth), not the subscription window. Contributor mode is globally enabled in settings.json. Owner follow-up measured: the explicit ID `--model muse-spark-1.3-contributor` ALSO works (OK rc=0, same recipe) — so both the plain alias with contributor mode inherited from settings and the explicit `-contributor` suffix are valid dispatch forms; the earlier note that \\\"contributor is only the mode, not part of the model ID\\\" was wrong. Consequence: spark-1.3 API dispatches are live regardless of the subscription window state; wonder-1.0 remains the subscription-lane model. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 owner relay — codex is available again\\nOwner relay: codex may be dispatched; the 06:10 relay's \\\"no new codex dispatches\\\" line is lifted. Route codex rounds per team.md and delegate-invocation-reference.md as usual. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 07:5x +07 — measured: muse headless root-caused on shiva; the recipe works on BOTH servers now\\nThe two shiva failure modes are now reproduced and fixed (entered in delegate-invocation-reference.md): (1) headless `muse exec` without `--trust-workspace` runs UNTRUSTED in any root not in ~/.config/muse/trust.json (only /home/sto/workspace/datomworld is listed server-side) — agent delegation silently off; a trivial probe still passes, so probe-only checks masked it; (2) the default --approval-mode on-request blocks forever headless with no API traffic — measured: a tool-using round hung to SIGTERM at 180s having done zero work. With `--trust-workspace --approval-mode never` a tool-using round completes in seconds; the sandbox (bwrap + user namespaces, both present on shiva AND jupiter) stays ON and the Bubblewrap banner is informational. Verified end-to-end on shiva and jupiter (muse 1.4.14, muse-wonder-1.0): headless muse work rounds are live on both servers. The 04:0x \\\"muse wedges silently on shiva\\\" entry is explained by mechanism (2), not an API outage. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 06:10 +07 — owner relay: muse, agy, glm, kimi are the only cli tools available\\n2026-11-03 03:10 +07 — measured: muse subscription window LIVE again (the 06:10 relay's muse lane is open)\\nProbe: `muse exec --model muse-wonder-1.0` with a fresh caller UUID returned \\\"OK\\\" (rc=0) at 03:10 +07 — earlier than the 20:41 window estimate recorded on 11-02; the reset must have been the daily window, not the 24h one. Consequence: muse is the default implementation destination NOW (with the standing kimi code-quality gate); agy stays out until its quota reset (~7 days from the 11-02 exhaustion). No new codex or mistral dispatches (the 06:10 relay). Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 03:2x +0700 — correction: agy is LIVE on both hosts (the earlier \\\"quota\\\" read was a per-session limit, not account exhaustion)\\nOwner challenged the agy-quota assumption. Measured: fresh `agy --model gemini-4-argon-high` probes return OK on the laptop and on shiva (rc=0, real responses). The 11-02 cljd round died with \\\"Individual quota reached, resets in 165h\\\" — that was the SESSION/turn budget of one 6,600-second 645k-token turn, not the account. Lesson (same class as the muse reset misread): a delegate CLI's own reset estimate is not availability; always probe with a fresh cheap session before ruling a destination out. agy is a live implementation destination NOW (with the standing kimi quality gate); the long-round budget stays a real constraint — split big rounds into resumed sessions rather than one turn. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 03:4x +0700 — amendment to the 03:2x correction: agy IS quota-limited after all (intermittent)\\nThe fresh-session probes passed at 03:1x-03:2x, but the dispatched real work round (xlang slice 1 on shiva) died instantly with \\\"Individual quota reached, resets in ~161h\\\", and an immediate laptop probe also returns quota-reached. True shape: the account pool is at its individual ceiling; small probes occasionally squeak through but a real work round cannot complete. AGY: unusable for work rounds until the reset (~161h); the probes' success was the tail of the pool, not availability. Implementation falls back to muse (live) for work rounds; kimi gate unchanged. The seat reverts the 03:2x \\\"live on both hosts\\\" conclusion to: live-for-probes, quota-walled-for-work. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 0x:xx +0700 — agy model-level routing (argon walled; claude/flash live); muse-on-shiva WORKS headless with --trust-workspace\\nOwner: \\\"argon has reached quota but gemini-3.8-flash is available and so are agy's claude models.\\\" Measured: claude-opus-5-5-high and gemini-3.8-flash-high probes return real responses; argon quota-walled (~161h). Implementation rounds route to agy claude-opus-5-5-high (hard rounds) / gemini-3.8-flash-high (light rounds); kimi quality gate unchanged. Separately measured: muse's Linux sandbox needs Bubblewrap user namespaces shiva cannot create — muse dispatched to a shiva worktree printed the sandbox warning and produced no work in 40 min. Muse on shiva: the earlier \\\"cannot sandbox\\\" read was WRONG — the Bubblewrap line is informational; the real gap was the missing --trust-workspace flag in my headless muse exec invocation (interactive muse in the repo trusts on its own; the owner verified both). Headless muse on shiva: `muse exec --trust-workspace --model muse-wonder-1.0 ...`. The reference recipe gains --trust-workspace for non-laptop roots. The xlang slice 1 round re-dispatched from muse to agy claude-opus-5-5-high on shiva. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 04:0x +0700 — agy quota is PER-MODEL: argon ~161h, claude-opus-5-5 ~4h, gemini-3.8-flash fresh; muse headless on shiva wedges silently\\nMeasured: the opus-5-5 finish-round dispatch died instantly with \\\"Individual quota reached, resets in 3h58m24s\\\" — a per-model window far shorter than argon's 161h. gemini-3.8-flash-high accepted a real round immediately after. Routing: agy models are individually probe-gated before dispatch (cheap probe per model, not per CLI); record each model's window as measured. Separately: the muse headless round on shiva made one burst of edits (good quality — the xlang conformance helper design + rebuilt cross_lang_test) then went silent 55 min with NO API usage on the owner's dev console; killed. Muse-on-shiva headless remains unreliable for work rounds (works interactively per the owner) — work rounds on shiva go to agy; muse work rounds stay on the laptop. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-03 08:1x +0700 — BLE-track seat: claude billing-walled; codex dispatch made against the 06:10 relay (violation recorded, no further codex)\\nMeasured this session: the `claude` CLI (flat Pro Max lane) now hard-fails with \\\"can't use your Claude subscription, usually because a payment is past due\\\" — treat claude as DOWN until the owner relays otherwise (the wrapper's own message names billing; per this file's rules that is a claim to re-verify, not permanent authority). Separately: this seat dispatched an r5 review to codex gpt-6.1-sol DESPITE the 06:10 owner relay (\\\"no new codex dispatches\\\") because it skipped checking this file before routing — the dispatch completed with a real verdict, which does not excuse the violation; recorded per the artifact protocol, and the verdict's findings are being reconciled regardless of the routing defect. Review confirmation rounds for the BLE track resume on allowed destinations only (muse / agy per-model-probe-gated / kimi gate), family-independent from the GLM-family implementer seat. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n\\n2026-11-03 15:55 +07 — owner relay: codex is available now\\nSupersedes the \\\"codex CLOSED until further notice\\\" entries (23:45 +07 Nov 2 and the \\\"codex is out\\\" note). Codex is back on the board. The model split from the argon/flash ruling stands: agy gemini-3.8-flash implements, argon reserved for hard problems/architectural review, kimi gates every implementation round. Codex is available for architect-review roles and implementation per the team.md policy. Entered by the yin.repl jolt orchestrator seat.\\n\\n2026-11-03 16:1x +07 — acknowledged: codex back on the board\\nThe yang.clojure seat read the 15:55 relay. Consequence: the H3-1 wedge diagnostic's next step (instrument encoder/forward-on-stream to locate the exact non-terminating loop) routes to codex engineering — codex did the strongest adversarial work on this track (the H3-0 review rounds) and the probe infrastructure is already on hardening3-h3-1 (8ea857c1) for it to build on. The kimi quality gate and the yin.vm-ruling boundary (no shared-runtime changes without a minimized reproduction) carry over unchanged. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n\\n2026-11-04 01:1x +07 — owner relay: kimi at 97.51%, resets 11-29; reroute to codex\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"kimi is at 97.51% usage and resets 11-29. reroute to codex\\\". Apply: kimi is quota-walled until 2026-11-29 — no new kimi dispatches (the pending gates on this track reroute to codex architect-review roles, which the 15:55 relay restored). The standing kimi quality gate moves to codex reviews for the duration; probe codex cheaply before each dispatch per the file's own rule. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-04 0x:xx +0700 — owner ruling: muse-1.3-contrib implements, gpt-6.1-sol is the architect\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"you're the orchestrator.md for this track. use muse-1.3-contrib to implement and gpt-6.1-sol as architect.md\\\". Apply: (1) implementation rounds on this track (yang.typescript / yang.php) route to muse model muse-1.3-contrib; (2) architect/design/review rounds route to codex gpt-6.1-sol (per the 15:55 Nov 3 relay, codex is available); (3) the standing muse/mistral quality gate is now sol's review (family-independent: sol reviews muse's work); (4) kimi remains available for architect duty but sol is the