{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1830,1910p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md; nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-DRAFT.md; rg -n 'occurrence|application|:app|contract' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | head -70\"","aggregated_output":"### 8.1 The module manifest\n\nA manifest is content: one map, materialized in `dao.jing`, named by its\naddress. It replaces the `{sym fn}` dictionary entry.\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.module/name        'my.lib\n :yin.module/schema      1                   ; this container's shape\n :yin.module/contracts   {:yin.ast/code          \"v3\"   ; per format\n                          :yin.semantic/code     \"v3\"\n                          :yin.debruijn.code     \"b2\"\n                          :yin.debruijn.register \"r2\"}\n :yin.module/tree        :segment/...        ; the one canonical tree\n :yin.module/derivations {:yin.semantic/code     :segment/...  ; record\n                          :yin.debruijn.code     :segment/...  ; record\n                          :yin.debruijn.register :segment/...} ; record\n :yin.module/index       {H :segment/... R :segment/...}       ; optional\n :yin.module/exports     #{'foo 'bar}\n :yin.module/requires    {'other.lib :segment/...}             ; manifest\n :yin.module/primitives  {'println :yin.k.pp/blake3-...}       ; profile\n :yin.module/footprint   {:store-keys #{} :effects #{}}}       ; UCF 7.6.1\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- **One tree.** `:yin.module/tree` is the root row id of the canonical\n  AST the module was authored as. The `:yin.ast/code` image *is* this\n  tree, so it has no derivation record.\n- **One derivation record per lowered format.** Each value under\n  `:yin.module/derivations` is the address of a content-addressed\n  derivation record in the shape `yin.vm.ledger/derive-record` mints:\n  `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree\n  :yin.ledger/output identity :yin.ledger/function f\n  :yin.ledger/profile {...}}`. The linker fetches the record (through a\n  `:yin.ledger/record` format record: `segment-matches?` identity, a\n  validator over these keys, no parts, no obligations) and checks\n  `:yin.ledger/input` equals `:yin.module/tree` and `:yin.ledger/output`\n  is the identity it then fetches. A record leading from a different\n  tree, or to a different identity, is `:derivation-mismatch`. Four\n  individually valid images from four different trees therefore cannot\n  share a manifest, and a swapped image fails before load.\n- **Three contracts, kept apart.** A manifest carries a schema version\n  and a per-format contract map, and neither is the other:\n  - `:yin.module/schema` versions the manifest container itself: which\n    keys exist and what they mean. It is checked by the\n    `:yin.module/manifest` format record's validator, which implements\n    exactly one schema version; a manifest of another version is\n    `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:schema`. It says nothing about any\n    bytecode.\n  - `:yin.module/contracts` maps each format the manifest lowered to\n    the execution contract that format's image targets: the UCF stamp\n    `\"v3\"` for the semantic vector and the tree, the stack lowering\n    contract `\"b2\"` for the stack image, the register contract `\"r2\"`\n    for the register image. These are the Rule R revisions; a manifest\n    naming \"v2\", \"b1\", or \"r1\" is `:contract-mismatch`, with no\n    migration. A format present under `:yin.module/derivations`\n    must be present here; one that is not is `:descriptor-defect` with\n    rule `:contract-missing`.\n  - A format record's `:contract` (section 4.1) is the one execution\n    contract that record implements, and a request's\n    `:yin.link/contract` is the one the requester runs.\n  **Contract before content.** Immediately after the manifest passes\n  steps 2 to 4, `(get :yin.module/contracts format)` for the requested\n  format is checked against the format record's `:contract`\n  (`:contract-mismatch`), before the name check and before any\n  derivation or image is fetched (section 4.2, step 0). A four-way\n  manifest linking through the stack kernel therefore compares `\"b2\"`\n  with `\"b2\"`, and the same manifest linking through the semantic\n  kernel compares `\"v3\"` with `\"v3\"`; the tree's `\"v3\"` and the register\n  image's `\"r2\"` play no part in either check. Revision r3 carried one\n  `:yin.module/contract \"v2\"` and would have refused every stack and\n  register link of a valid manifest; that was a defect.\n- **Verifying and trusting are two policies, and verifying never\n  trusts.** The linker's `:derivation` policy is one of `:verifying` or\n  `:trusted`, chosen by the composition; there is no policy under which\n  a verifying link accepts a claim it did not recompute.\n  - Under `:verifying`, for every image it delivers the linker fetches\n    the manifest's tree through the `:yin.ast/code` record (steps 2 to\n    4, so the tree itself is verified, and no further: the tree's own\n    obligations are the walker's business, scanned only when the tree is\n    the requested image), re-lowers it under the exact\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-05 04:30:00 GMT\n     2\tStatus: ADDENDUM A to the signed attribution-as-query ruling (1794600000000-...ruling-of-record.md) — DRAFT, pending gpt-6-astra review, then owner signature\n     3\t# ADDENDUM A — call-site annotation generalizes: every yang.* compilation pipeline annotates the call site\n     4\t\n     5\t## The owner's directive (recorded verbatim in intent)\n     6\t\n     7\t\"the other compilers must also add more information to the compilation pipeline by annotating the callsite.\" The site provenance defined by this ruling — caller-site identity at application expressions, post-macroexpansion — is hereby the **declared standard for every yang.* frontend**, not an oracle-local mechanism. The oracle's instantiation (in flight) is the first consumer; the other frontends' adoption is scheduled direction, not a hypothetical.\n     8\t\n     9\t## What generalizes, and to where\n    10\t\n    11\t1. **The semantics generalize unchanged**: caller-site identity, assigned at application expressions post-macroexpansion, carried on the callee-entry record; consumers resolve sites against declared/derived tables. The oracle's R2 mechanism is instantiation #1 of exactly this contract.\n    12\t2. **The representation home generalizes to UAST**: for frontends to emit site ids natively, the UAST application-node representation gains the annotation (a §8.1 contract revision — the linker's derivation/contract machinery already versions formats). This is the architectural difference between instantiation #1 (harness-level, oracle-owned) and the generalization (representation-level, VM-owned) — and it needs its own design round before any frontend lands it.\n    13\t3. **The second consumer is now SCHEDULED, which unblocks R1's deferred half**: the durable correspondence record (R1's descoped address-linked adaptation manifest) lands alongside the generalization — its trigger condition (\"a second consumer exists\") is satisfied by this directive by owner declaration.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Sequencing (proposed)\n    16\t\n    17\t1. The oracle round (in flight) lands instantiation #1 — unchanged by this addendum.\n    18\t2. The generalization DESIGN round: the UAST application-node annotation (representation, contracts revision, per-frontend lowering changes, budget reviews per track) — architect-reviewed before any frontend dispatches.\n    19\t3. Per-frontend adoption rounds (typescript, php, python; jolt inherits through the portable sources) — each with its own gate; the cell-ref sweeps and all current lanes are unaffected and proceed unchanged.\n    20\t4. R1's durable correspondence record lands with (2)/(3) — additive to the oracle's site tables, per R1's condition (b) (the versioned evidence format).\n    21\t\n    22\t## Boundaries\n    23\t\n    24\t- No existing rule changes: the signed ruling's R1-R7 stand as written; this addendum schedules the generalization and unblocks R1's deferred half.\n    25\t- The in-flight sweeps (cell-ref, ts/php/python), the jolt J2 re-run, and the oracle round are unaffected.\n    26\t- Budget impact per frontend is a design-round deliverable, not assumed here.\n    27\t\n    28\t## What astra is asked to review\n    29\t\n    30\t1. Is the UAST application-node representation the right home for the annotation (vs per-frontend lowering-level tags)? What does the §8.1 contracts revision require?\n    31\t2. Does scheduling R1's durable record against the generalization hold, or should it precede/follow differently?\n    32\t3. Per-frontend sequencing and budget-review obligations — anything the design round must measure first?\n    33\t4. Any interaction with the in-flight seats' work (the sweeps, the J2 re-run, the jolt host) that this addendum must call out?\n    34\t5. Verdict: endorse the addendum for owner signature, or amend.\n18:[`datom.md`](./datom.md) (the datom contract: stream-local `e`/`t`/`m`,\n23:(UCF: the canonical instruction vector §7.3.2, contract stamp §7.3.3,\n43:historical; its stance and contract history survive in the renamed\n59:|                | the Map AST semantic root      | a linearized bytecode of flat       | address — a row's  | address                  | name, no occurrence, no        |\n73:|                |                                | and the occurrence relation (§6.1)  |                    |                          |                                |\n90:and the occurrence relation (`§6.1`) — and wherever this document says\n106:| provenance and custody | derive, expand, and publish events, UCF's occurrence ledgers and leases: facts a medium possesses; a hash possesses nothing        |\n125:- **Nothing about time, naming, occurrence, or provenance enters code\n140:- **Content identity is not occurrence identity.** An address says *what*\n142:  expansion attempt produced it is occurrence information, and it is kept\n163: :body {:type :application,\n169:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\n182:[B :application C [D E] true]\n246:| `key`   | a store key: any value of the    | the store contract is a map lookup with no key restriction (`ast_walker.cljc:401`, `:402-404`); the linearizer admits any |\n288:| :application            | 4          | [:application operator operands      | operator operands tail?  | node, nodes, bool| :371-376           | :424-429        |\n330:**`tail?` is a slot of `:application` only.** The one reader of the tail\n332:(`linearize.cljc:107-108`), which reads it on `:application`; the walker\n339:mark on an `:if`'s branches is carried by the branch `:application` nodes\n361:| `:application` tail?                               | `false`                             | codec emits only `true` (`v2.cljc:412`); the linearizer reads `(boolean …)` |\n364:| `:application` / `:dao.stream.apply/call` operands | `[]`                                | walker `ast_walker.cljc:383`                                                |\n367:### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\n384:  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\n405:- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\n411:  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\n426:An **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\n430:is keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\n436:removes. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\n453:| harvest catalogue      | `[[:source medium batch] h j path]`: occurrence `[j path]` belongs to the `h`-th **original definition** in the        | the frontend or the  |\n512:  (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`), while an application interprets an\n519:  effect descriptor under the applicable execution contract**; a result\n520:  the contract would interpret is stored by the arm and executed by the\n568:| lowering as an address-to-address function    | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and each application of it is one derivation record (§5.2)                        |\n596:contract precisely and its remaining residuals: scalar (symbol) metadata\n636:### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\n645:`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\n659:\"This call site\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\n661:joins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\n663:or a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\n665:occurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\n683:occurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\n696:cosmetic; the no-fallthrough contract (`:variable`'s lexical-only\n715:`:application`'s operator/operands slots), not generic over §2.3's full\n725:at one occurrence and bound at another — and this is not a permanent\n728:free at one occurrence and bound at another (e.g. `((fn [x] x) x)`, whose\n733:which would silently drop the genuinely free occurrence from a computed\n741:*specific occurrence* is exactly a places-not-contents question, and\n742:§6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\n744:each occurrence's own path to its ancestors, not the row's abstracted\n746:`occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\n748:occurrence relation of §2.5's shape as `yin.vm/occurrences` emits it, a\n754:richer, occurrence-joined query, exactly as this section claims.\n756:**Root-scoped, and portable data.** A production occurrence relation\n757:holds every reachable tree's occurrences together, and structural paths\n761:misclassify an occurrence in one tree against a binder in another;\n764:`yin.vm/occurrence-rules`, is one non-recursive rule over explicitly\n778:A name is bound at `[root path]` iff a `:lambda` occurrence **of the same\n790:`occurrence-walk-covers-the-whole-grammar` resolves binding through `:if`\n792:emits the occurrence tuples is `yin.vm/occurrences`.\n802:slot of a two-operand application whose first operand is a literal\n803:symbol other than `yin/def`; every other occurrence, a `:lambda` binding\n826:strictly retain named variables. Therefore, every contract name in this\n836:tree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\n850:                       :yin.code/contract \"v3\"  ; UCF S7.3.3 contract\n861:UCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\n864:two deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\n873:| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\n882:| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\n886:contract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\n888:execution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\n907:   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
