{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"agent_message","text":"**gpt-6-astra’s verdict: amend before owner signature.** The direction is endorsed; the draft needs four clarifications.\n\n1. **Attach provenance to UAST application occurrences; do not yet prescribe a canonical node field.** Existing architecture defines a call site as `[origin root-address path]` and excludes provenance from hashed code content. Occurrence side tables and instruction-provenance mappings already exist. The draft’s representation choice conflicts with that boundary. See [occurrence identity](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:659).\n\n   Replace item 2 with:\n\n   > Every frontend preserves caller-site provenance for post-expansion UAST application occurrences through a shared, language-neutral occurrence contract. The design round starts from existing occurrence and instruction-provenance relations. This addendum does not authorize a new canonical AST slot; any such proposal requires explicit justification and amendment of the occurrence/content boundary.\n\n2. **Separate the versioning obligations.** “A §8.1 contract revision” is too broad. Evidence schemas, lowering profiles, execution contracts, and module-manifest schemas have distinct revision rules. Existing machinery detects incompatibility; it does not automatically migrate artifacts. See [§8.1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1871) and [lowering profiles](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:861).\n\n   Require the design round to inventory affected producers, validators, lowering and reconstruction paths, derivation verification, publication, persisted artifacts, and mismatch behavior. Revise only the contracts actually changed.\n\n3. **Make adoption coverage and host verification explicit.** Include production `yang.clojure`: oracle instrumentation does not establish coverage of ordinary compilation paths. Inventory other `yang.*` entry points. Jolt inherits shared implementation changes, but still requires an applicable load/parity gate.\n\n   Replace the rollout wording with:\n\n   > Adoption covers production Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, and Python, with additional entry points inventoried during design. Each frontend has an acceptance gate; applicable JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt host lanes verify the resulting revision. Current sweeps, the J2 rerun, and the oracle round continue under their existing scopes.\n\n   Also describe instantiation #1 as **oracle-specific instrumentation**, since its signed scope already permits walker changes.\n\n4. **Activate R1 at signature and establish a precise dependency.** Owner scheduling validly activates R1; an already shipped second consumer is unnecessary. However, “lands with (2)/(3)” leaves acceptance ordering unclear.\n\n   Replace that wording with:\n\n   > By signing this addendum, the owner activates R1’s deferred work. The design round specifies the durable correspondence record and its additive relationship to versioned oracle evidence. Implementation and independent reconstruction complete before, or atomically with, acceptance of the first additional consumer that relies on it. The oracle round remains unblocked.\n\nThe design round should explicitly measure application counts, provenance and trace sizes, peak trace occupancy, instruction overhead, compilation/loading/query time, and memory. Separate static annotation costs from runtime tracing costs, and declare bounds before tagged runs. Its correctness gates should cover duplicate occurrences, macro expansion, helper calls, indirect and tail calls, and applicable suspension/resumption paths.\n\n**With these amendments, Astra endorses the addendum for owner signature as architectural direction and scheduling authority.** R1–R7 remain intact. No files were changed; this was a read-only review."}}
